<binaryimage arch="ARM:LE:32:v8:default">
<!--
    kuna: a register the function sets for a system register is not the
    high word of its return.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 return recovery, the output trials of the
    function's own RETURN (ActionReturnRecovery, p4_calls/kuna_retsysreg.rs).
    A strict fix: no option.

    Source (clang -target armv7a-linux-gnueabihf -marm -O2 -fno-inline at
    0x1000, clang -target thumbv7em-none-eabihf -mcpu=cortex-m4 -O2
    -fno-inline at 0x1800; bytes verbatim below, call relocations applied):

        typedef unsigned long long u64;
        int g(void);  u64 g64(void);
        int keepc(void) { int r = g();
          __asm volatile("vmsr fpscr, %0" :: "r"(0x3000000)); return r; }
        int comp(int a) { int r = a * 3;
          __asm volatile("vmsr fpscr, %0" :: "r"(0x3000000)); return r; }
        int compm(int a) { int r = a * 3;
          __asm volatile("msr cpsr_c, %0" :: "r"(0x13)); return r; }
        u64 callhi(void) { u64 v = g64() | 0xff00000000ULL;
          __asm volatile("vmsr fpscr, %0" :: "r"((unsigned)(v >> 32)));
          return v; }
        u64 mcrr_ret(u64 x) { u64 v = x * 7;
          __asm volatile("mcrr p15, 0, %Q0, %R0, c2" :: "r"(v)); return v; }

        Thumb: keepc_t is keepc again; raise_bp is FreeRTOS's
        ulPortRaiseBASEPRI (mrs r0,basepri; mov.w r1,#0x50; msr basepri,r1;
        isb; dsb; bx lr); lock_prim is mrs r0,primask; movs r1,#1;
        msr primask,r1; bx lr.

    keepc is bl g; mov r1,#0x3000000; vmsr fpscr,r1; pop {r11,pc}.

    The bug: return recovery takes a register copied into fpscr or cpsr, or
    handed to a CALLOTHER such as setBasePriority, for one used only by the
    RETURN, so r1 joined r0 as a 64-bit return whose high word is the system
    register's operand: CONCAT44(0x3000000,g()), CONCAT44(0x50,v1).

    The fix: a second return register whose value is handed to a system
    register is not returned.  comp, compm, raise_bp and lock_prim return
    the 32-bit value; keepc and keepc_t return nothing, as a bare
    bl g; pop {r11,pc} does until a caller reads the result.  callhi (the
    high word of g64's own result) and mcrr_ret (both words written to one
    64-bit register) keep the pair.
-->
